Antony Charles Robert Armstrong-Jones, 1st Earl of Snowdon, <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Victorian_Order">GCVO</a> <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Designers_for_Industry">RDI</a> (born 7 March 1930) is an English <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photographer">photographer</a> and filmmaker. He was married to <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princess_Margaret,_Countess_of_Snowdon">Princess Margaret</a>, younger daughter of <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_VI">King George VI</a>, and younger sister of <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_II">Queen Elizabeth II</a>.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;
<p class="MsoNormal">Armstrong-Jones also co-designed, in 1960–1963, with <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Newby">Frank Newby</a> and <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cedric_Price">Cedric Price</a>, the aviary of the <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Zoo">London Zoo</a>. He also had a major role in designing the physical arrangements for the 1969 investiture of his nephew Prince Charles as <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prince_of_Wales">Prince of Wales</a>.</p>

John Quentin Hejduk (19 July 1929 – 3 July 2000) was an American architect, artist and educator of <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Czechs">Czech</a> origin who spent much of his life in <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City">New York City</a>, <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USA">USA</a>. Hejduk is noted for his use of attractive and often difficult-to-construct objects and shapes; also for a profound interest in the fundamental issues of shape, organization, representation, and reciprocity.

&nbsp;
<p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;">Hejduk studied at the <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooper_Union_School_of_Art_and_Architecture">Cooper Union School of Art and Architecture</a>, the <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Cincinnati">University of Cincinnati</a>, and the <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_Graduate_School_of_Design">Harvard Graduate School of Design</a>, from which he graduated with a Masters in Architecture in 1953. He worked in several offices in New York including that of <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I._M._Pei">I. M. Pei</a> and Partners and the office of A.M. Kinney and Associates. He established his own practice in New York in 1965.</p>

With Battersea Power Station’s future once again in doubt, its new owner might wish it could add Cedric Price’s Bathat scheme to the list of options for the site.

&nbsp;

Price argued that trying to preserve it was the wrong approach. In 1984, when its owner held a limited architectural competition for the site, he launched his counterproposal in BD. This involved removing the lateral brick walls — the source of most maintenance problems — while supporting the upper levels and four chimneys on the existing steel structure. He pointed out that a signifi-cant part of the building had little historical value and was not worth the huge conser-vation costs, but by keeping the control room and chimneys as a celebrated symbol of London’s skyline, the land could be be released for redevelopment. He was deliberately vague on what could go on the site, believing an architect’s role was only to widen options not narrow them.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

